  "name": "Mozambique (1960s Havana percussion dance)",
  "family": "global",
  "lineage": "Dance rhythm created in Havana in 1963 by the percussionist Pedro Izquierdo, known as Pello el Afrokán, who drew on the conga and comparsa drumming of carnival and led a large band of congas, bass drums and cowbells with trumpets and trombones, a lead singer and an answering chorus. Hits such as María Caracoles made it a national dance craze in the mid-1960s. A generic membrane-drum card stands in for the bass drums.",
